{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' verification/example_site/spaces/mind/sources/artifacts/source-03-the-role-of-alpha-band-brain-oscillations-as-a-sensory-suppression-mechanism-dur-w2005105933--7b6b7d5bc8b9/source.md\"","aggregated_output":"# 03-the-role-of-alpha-band-brain-oscillations-as-a-sensory-suppression-mechanism-dur--w2005105933\n\nReview Article\n                                                                                                                                  published: 05 July 2011\n                                                                                                                          doi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2011.00154\n\n\n\nThe role of alpha-band brain oscillations as a sensory\nsuppression mechanism during selective attention\nJohn J. Foxe1,2* and Adam C. Snyder1,2\n1\n  The Cognitive Neurophysiology Laboratory, Children's Evaluation and Rehabilitation Center, Department of Pediatrics and Dominick P. Purpura Department of\n  Neuroscience, Albert Einstein College of Medicine, Bronx, NY, USA\n2\n  The Cognitive Neurophysiology Laboratory, Program in Cognitive Neuroscience, Departments of Psychology and Biology, City College of the City University of\n  New York, New York, NY, USA\n\n\n\nEdited by:                                Evidence has amassed from both animal intracranial recordings and human electrophysiology\nGregor Thut, University of Glasgow, UK\n                                          that neural oscillatory mechanisms play a critical role in a number of cognitive functions such\nReviewed by:                              as learning, memory, feature binding and sensory gating. The wide availability of high-density\nGregor Thut, University of Glasgow, UK\n                                          electrical and magnetic recordings (64–256 channels) over the past two decades has allowed for\n*Correspondence:\nJohn J. Foxe, Cognitive\n                                          renewed efforts in the characterization and localization of these rhythms. A variety of cognitive\nNeurophysiology Laboratory, Children's    effects that are associated with specific brain oscillations have been reported, which range\nEvaluation and Rehabilitation Center,     in spectral, temporal, and spatial characteristics depending on the context. Our laboratory\nDepartments of Pediatrics and             has focused on investigating the role of alpha-band oscillatory activity (8–14 Hz) as a potential\nNeuroscience, Albert Einstein College\nof Medicine, 1225 Morris Park Avenue,\n                                          attentional suppression mechanism, and this particular oscillatory attention mechanism will be\nSuite 1-C Bronx, NY 10461 USA.            the focus of the current review. We discuss findings in the context of intersensory selective\ne-mail: john.foxe@einstein.yu.edu         attention as well as intrasensory spatial and feature-based attention in the visual, auditory, and\n                                          tactile domains. The weight of evidence suggests that alpha-band oscillations can be actively\n                                          invoked within cortical regions across multiple sensory systems, particularly when these regions\n                                          are involved in processing irrelevant or distracting information. That is, a central role for alpha\n                                          seems to be as an attentional suppression mechanism when objects or features need to be\n                                          specifically ignored or selected against.\n                                          Keywords: alpha, brain, oscillations, selection, attention, biasing, suppression, EEG\n\n\n\n    “Sit in reverie, and watch the changing color of the waves that break           density multi-channel electro- and magneto- encephalographic\n    upon the idle seashore of the mind.”                                            recordings (EEG and MEG) have allowed for unprecedented\n    \b                                    -Henry Wadsworth Longfellow                advances in our understanding of the physiology of human selec-\nThe ability to selectively attend to a specific subset of one’s sen-                tive attention. This report specifically addresses evidence for the\nsory realm is at the root of human cognition and negotiation                        role of oscillatory brain mechanisms in selective attention (e.g.,\nof one’s environment. The alternative, whereby all information                      Vanni et al., 1997; Foxe et al., 1998; Gruber et al., 1999; Shibata\nin one’s sensory domain is processed to the level of conscious-                     et al., 1999; Sokolov et al., 1999; Worden et al., 2000; Sauseng\nness, does not allow for normal function and may be partially                       et al., 2005; Yamagishi et al., 2005; Kelly et al., 2006; Thut et al.,\ninvolved in certain neural disorders such as Autism spectrum                        2006; Rihs et al., 2007; Snyder and Foxe, 2010).\ndisorders (cf. Markram and Markram, 2010) and attention deficit                         Two basic varieties of attentionally mediated, selective modu-\ndisorder (Armstrong et al., 2001). By necessity, successful task                    lation of the neural circuitry involved in processing a particular\ncompletion in daily life employs selective attention to one extent                  stimulus array can be envisioned. The first, and by far the most\nor another. By inference, the ability to attend is assumed to be                    often reported, involves the selective enhancement of neural\na “capacity-limited” system and to some degree, humans have                         responsiveness or efficacy in those cells responsible for processing\nexecutive or conscious control over what aspects of their environ-                  the particular stimulus upon which a subject’s attention is focused,\nment are tapping this limited system from moment-to-moment                          whether that stimulus is already present or merely anticipated (e.g.,\n(e.g., Broadbent, 1958; Kahneman, 1973; Neely, 1977; Schneider                      Moran and Desimone, 1985; Haenny and Schiller, 1988; Corbetta\nand Shiffrin, 1977; Shiffrin and Schneider, 1977; Schneider and                     et al., 1993; Luck et al., 1997). The second variety of attentional\nFisk, 1982). This endogenous form of attention is distinct from                     modulation involves selective inhibition or disengagement of those\nattention that is captured by salient stimuli in the environment                    neurons responsible for processing stimuli outside of the focus of\nin an involuntary fashion (exogenous attention). How the brain                      attention, so that competing or distracting information is not or\nachieves endogenous attentional selectivity has been a matter of                    will not be processed fully (e.g., Moran and Desimone, 1985; Motter,\nvigorous research for some decades now. Advances in neuroimag-                      1993; Foxe et al., 1998; Reynolds et al., 1999; Vanduffel et al., 2000;\ning technologies such as the hemodynamic functional imaging                         Worden et al., 2000). This would involve placing the neural cir-\nmethodologies (fMRI and PET) and the ability to conduct high-                       cuitry normally responsible for processing such distracters in some\n\n\n\n\nwww.frontiersin.org                                                                                                            July 2011 | Volume 2 | Article 154 | 1\n\nFoxe and Snyder                                                                                                                 Alpha and attention\n\n\n\n\nform of inhibited or decreased receptivity state. Evidence from          mechani