          "body": "Across the sources, cortical organization matters both for conscious experience and for selective attention, but the claims operate at different explanatory levels. Information integration theory addresses the conditions for conscious experience, while alpha-band attention research addresses suppressive modulation of sensory processing."
        },
        {
          "title": "Integrated Conscious Systems",
          "body": "Information integration theory argues that consciousness depends on complexes with high integrated information. A distributed thalamocortical network is presented as well suited for consciousness because it combines functional specialization with functional integration."
        },
        {
          "title": "Attention Networks and Alpha Control",
          "body": "Alpha-band attention research describes suppressive modulation as primarily cortico-cortical, involving frontal and parietal networks, with possible thalamo-cortical contributions when distracting sensory processing must be reduced. Higher alpha is linked to lower visual excitability and weaker detection."
        },
        {
          "title": "Synthesis Boundary",
          "body": "A cautious synthesis is that conscious access and attentional suppression both depend on organized interactions among specialized cortical systems. The provided evidence does not establish alpha oscillations as a direct measure of integrated information or consciousness."
